En/na willh ha escrit:
> On Aug 24, 5:54 am, Jordi Guillaumes i Pons <jguillai...@hotmail.com>
> 
> System: 660QR, VAX/VAXserver 4000 Model 200
> BA215 Enclosure
> 
> Fan Assembly------70-25454-01
> Fan, Tube Axial 4.5 inches-----12-23609-04
> Power Supply 120V------H7868-A
> Power Supply 240V------H7868-B

Well, my old 3300 blows again. Finally, there were TWO problems, not one.

First, one of the fans was dead. I finally disassembled the whole 
assembly (the connectors are tricky, and that cable which goes from the 
on-off switch to the AC conditioner is... well, I can't find a word for 
it ;)), and found one of the blowers was dead (opened circuit, ohmeter 
out of scale), and the other seemed to be OK.

So... why neither of both was functioning? I decided to apply the 
scientific method (also known as try-and-error). I bought one el-cheapo 
chinese blower electrically and physically compatible with the original, 
and replaced the dead one. I reassembled the whole thing, put it back in 
the bottom of the BA125, put it back the power supply and...

Nothing! All silence! The fans were not moving.

Once again, out with the power supply, out with the fan assembly, 
disconnect the cable from the backplane. Multimeter in hand, I tested 
the circuit from the backplane connector and it seemed to be OK for both 
fans.

Next step: I found a schemtics of the pin distribution of the power 
source connection in the backplane, so I checked the paths between tha 
connector and the fan assembly cable connector. All four of them were OK.

Next step: connect again the cable (this time I did'nt put back the 
assembly in...) and check the circuits FROM the backplane connector.

Well, none of both was working. Ohmeter out of scale.

Then, check the circuit from the backplane connector to each fan thru 
the cable. And here was the problem: two of the four cables didn't get 
connected. One of each fan, of course :).

So, the problem was in the connector at the end of the cable coming from 
the fan assembly and going to the backplane.

Solution? Well. I'll have to get another cable (the connector is closed, 
one piece of plastic). But in the meantime I droped a little, thin 
copper whire inside each hole of the connector. Put it back in place, 
re-assembled the whole thinkg, crossed my fingers. reconnected the mains 
and pushed the ON switch, and...

WOOOOOOSSSSSSHHHHH!!! The blowers blowed again!

So I'm happy I've put back in order my 3300. Now, I have to locate the 
P/N for the cable (I have one P/N for the 213 enclosure, but I'm not 
sure at all that is the same one), get one and forget this blowing 
issue. And (perhaps) buy a real fan to substitute the cheap chinese one 
I put in...

Thanks for your patience, and for the tips you provided!



